<section class="hero project-hero">
  <h1>{{ site.name }}</h1>
  <p>{{ site.tagline }}</p>
  <p class="cta">
    <a class="button" href="{{ '/get-started/' | url }}">Get started</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="{{ '/devices/' | url }}">See devices</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="{{ site.repo }}" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    gemifi is open-source firmware for MIDI controllers, in the spirit of
    ESPHome. Describe the hardware in a YAML file, build the firmware, and
    flash it from the browser. Use it to revive or repurpose a cheap keyboard,
    or to build your own controller on an ESP32.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Describe it in YAML</h3>
        <p>Keys, buttons, pads, faders, knobs, encoders, LEDs, displays and expander chips, each with a pin and a default MIDI message. <a href="{{ '/components/' | url }}">What it can describe</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Build it in the dashboard</h3>
        <p>Edit and check the YAML in a web dashboard that runs from one Docker image, then build the firmware with one click.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Flash it from the browser</h3>
        <p>Install updates over Web MIDI or Web Serial, watch the device's log, and change what each control sends without rebuilding.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol class="flow">
    <li>
      <span class="flow-step">Device YAML</span>
      <span class="flow-note"><code>devices/&lt;device&gt;.yaml</code>: what's wired to which pin</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="flow-step">Builder and dashboard</span>
      <span class="flow-note">Docker image <code>{{ site.image }}</code>: checks the YAML, generates the tables, compiles</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="flow-step">Web updater</span>
      <span class="flow-note">Flashes the image over Web MIDI (USB) or Web Serial, shows logs, edits settings</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="flow-step">Your controller</span>
      <span class="flow-note">A class-compliant MIDI device that also speaks gemifi's SysEx protocol</span>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      On a commercial keyboard, gemifi needs a debug probe once, to put its
      small USB bootloader on the chip. After that every update goes over USB
      from the browser. ESP chips have a bootloader built in, so they need no
      probe at all.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>The pieces</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Everything is in one repository, and every part speaks the same SysEx
    protocol.
  </p>
  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ site.blob }}tools/builder/README.md" rel="noopener">Builder</a></h3>
        <p>Checks a device YAML, turns it into C tables and builds the firmware. Comes with the dashboard, in one Docker image.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>tools/builder</code></span><span>Used for every build</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ '/components/' | url }}">Core firmware</a></h3>
        <p>The controller itself, the same on every chip: scanning, velocity, LEDs, presets, settings, DIN MIDI and DAW feedback.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>core/</code></span><span>Runs on two keyboards</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ '/platforms/' | url }}">Platforms</a></h3>
        <p>The chip-specific layer for STM32F1, Nuvoton NUC1xx, and ESP32 / ESP8266: start-up, USB or serial MIDI, flash and updates.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>platform/</code></span><span>ESP not yet on hardware</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ site.blob }}bootloader/README.md" rel="noopener">Bootloaders</a></h3>
        <p>Small USB-MIDI bootloaders for the NUC1xx (in its 4 KB boot flash) and the STM32F1 (the first 9 KB). They take new apps from the browser and never write themselves.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>bootloader/</code></span><span>Both run on hardware</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ site.blob }}protocol/sysex.md" rel="noopener">SysEx protocol</a></h3>
        <p>One protocol over USB, serial or BLE MIDI for device info, logs, settings, presets, firmware uploads and DAW scripts.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>protocol/</code></span><span>Version 1</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ site.blob }}web/updater/README.md" rel="noopener">Web updater</a></h3>
        <p>The browser page: flash firmware, read the device's log, edit its settings and presets, try host feedback. Chrome or Edge.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>web/updater</code></span><span>In use on both keyboards</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3><a class="card-link" href="{{ '/daw-setup/' | url }}">DAW scripts</a></h3>
        <p>Ableton Live and Bitwig scripts, and a Zynthian driver, that learn a device's controls and drive its LEDs, rings and display.</p>
        <div class="card-foot"><span><code>hosts/</code></span><span>Not yet tried in the DAWs</span></div>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>A device in YAML</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Part of the description of the M-Audio Keystation Mini 32 MK3. The format
    follows ESPHome's conventions for pins, ids and names.
  </p>
  <div class="prose wide">
<pre class="code"><code>{% raw %}gemifi: 2
name: keystation-mini32
friendly_name: Keystation Mini 32
project:
  version: 0.1.9
platform: nuvoton-nuc1xx
chip: NUC120LC1BN
usb: { vid: 0x1209, pid: 0x0002 }
scan_interval: 125us
boot_combo: [pitch_down, pitch_up]

buttons:
  - { id: sustain, pin: { number: PA1, inverted: true }, action: cc_momentary, cc: 64, led: sustain_led }
  - { id: oct_down, name: Oct -, pin: { number: PA3, inverted: true }, action: octave_down }
  - { id: pitch_down, pin: { number: PA5, inverted: true }, action: bend_down }

leds:
  - { id: sustain_led, pin: { number: PA12, inverted: true } }

analog:
  - { id: volume, pin: PA0, cc: 7, adc_min: 32, adc_max: 4064 }

key_matrix:
  type: velocity
  rows: [PA8, PA9, PC0, PC1, PC2, PC3, PC6, PC7]
  columns: [PB0, PB1, PB2, PB3, PB4, PB5, PB8, PB12]
  keys: 32
  first_note: 48
  velocity: { fast: 1.6ms, slow: 120ms }{% endraw %}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <p class="more">
    <a href="{{ site.blob }}devices/keystation-mini32-mk3.yaml" rel="noopener">The full file</a> ·
    <a href="{{ '/components/' | url }}">What a YAML can describe</a> ·
    <a href="{{ site.blob }}core/README.md#device-yaml" rel="noopener">The YAML format</a>
  </p>
</section>

